    "rationale_critique": "1) The core diagnosis conflicts with the data. The rationale says the failure is a steady bias that proportional gain cannot remove, so the offset should scale as bias/(kp x slope). But iteration 0 (kp 3, effective slope about 125/m) and iteration 1 (kp 3, slope about 31/m) differ about 4x in gain, and max_error barely moved (0.0614 and 0.0609 on s-bend and slalom, 0.0614 to 0.0631 on oval). A gain-dependent offset model does not fit that. The draft should explain why max_error is nearly gain-independent, or the bias model is unsupported. 2) The claim that ki = 0.3 cancels the mismatch is unverified. The integral is limited to +/-2, so its maximum contribution is 0.3 x 2 = 0.6 turn units. At the draft's slope of about 62 turn per metre, that is equivalent to about 1 cm of lateral offset. The draft does not show that the bias is smaller than this, and once the integral saturates it stops correcting. 3) The rationale says the effect of each gain change 'can be read directly', but it changes kp, ki, kd, base_speed and mass together, and earlier iterations also changed sensor y and wheel_track. Any race result therefore cannot be attributed to one change. 4) The expected results (max_error below 0.05, progress well above 0.2, no line loss) are stated as outcomes, not hypotheses, and no calculation supports them. 5) The progress claim is inconsistent with the time budget. The draft equates base_speed with ground speed, but the contract only sets PWM fraction (base_speed/2), and wheel lag, drag and slip reduce ground speed further. It also says progress near 1.0 is worth about 250 points, but progress reaches 0.995 only by finishing, so near-1.0 without completion is hard to reach in 300 steps. The draft should say which measurement would show ground speed. 6) The fallback ('lower kp to about 1.5 or raise ki') does not say what value of which measurement triggers it, or how a line-loss event count or oscillation value would change the choice. 7) The mass reduction to 0.6 kg is justified only as 'faster response', with no yaw or speed calculation, and it was not tested in isolation.",
    "design_critique": "1) Raising kd to 0.8 is contradicted by the last race. Iteration 1 raised kd from 0.3 to 0.6 and steering_oscillation on the oval rose from 0.075 to 0.138, with line_loss_events rising from 1.0 to 1.55. The draft raises kd again, to 0.8, with no evidence it helps. Derivative acts on the change in signal per second. A sensor-edge step of about 0.5 signal units in one 10 ms step gives about 50/s, times 0.8, about 40 turn units (about 14000 PWM counts). Noise and edge crossings therefore dominate the derivative term. A kd of 0.8 is likely the main cause of the jitter. 2) kp = 2.0 is a 33 percent reduction from kp 3, but the surviving failure is still early on every track. This is a small change, and the draft gives no test that would distinguish a gain-limited loop from a bias. 3) ki = 0.3 with a +/-2 integral cap is weak authority (see rationale point 2), and the integral of the signal, not the error, is a poor bias estimator when the robot sits off-centre. 4) base_speed 1.3 gives up the 500-point completion bonus and the time term, and the draft concedes completion is unlikely. Progress without completion is worth at most about 250 points times progress, so the draft trades a possible completion for a small progress gain. It needs a stated reason that the robot can stay on track at a higher speed, or a second test at 1.6 with kd near 0.05. 5) The mass change to 0.6 kg reduces yaw inertia but also changes the drive response, and the draft does not show the effect on the off-track margin. 6) A better next design would test one change at a time: kd near 0.05, kp 2.0, ki 0, base 1.4. That would show whether the failure follows the derivative or the proportional term, and it is cheap in race runs.",
